<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Subject–Verb Agreement - Joined subjects</h1>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>Joined by and</h2>
    <p>Two subjects joined by <em>and</em> usually take a plural verb. Tom and Jerry <strong>are</strong> friends. A pen and a pencil <strong>are</strong> on the table.</p>
    <p>When the words name one thing or one idea, a singular verb may be used. Bread and butter <strong>is</strong> my favourite. Fish and chips <strong>is</strong> popular.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-blue">
    <h2>Joined by or or nor</h2>
    <p>The verb usually agrees with the nearer subject.</p>
    <p>Either the teacher or the students <strong>are</strong> coming.</p>
    <p>Either the students or the teacher <strong>is</strong> coming.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-pink">
    <h2>Groups and titles</h2>
    <p>A collective noun names a group: <em>team, family, group, crowd, committee</em>. When you mean the group as one unit, use a singular verb. The team <strong>is</strong> winning. My family <strong>is</strong> travelling.</p>
    <p>In British English, the same noun can take a plural verb when you mean the members. When you are first practising, choose a subject that is clearly singular or clearly plural.</p>
    <p>The title of a book or film is singular, even when it contains <em>and</em>. “Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire” <strong>is</strong> my favourite.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
    <h2>Check, then choose</h2>
    <p><strong>1.</strong> Find the main verb. <strong>2.</strong> Find the subject and its head noun. <strong>3.</strong> Ignore the words between them. <strong>4.</strong> Decide if the subject is singular or plural, then choose the matching verb.</p>
    <p>“The keys in the drawer (is / are) missing.” What is missing? <strong>The keys</strong>. <em>Keys</em> is plural, so choose <strong>are</strong>.</p>
  </section>
</div>
